---

pancake lets two programs talk the way well-behaved objects do. One side hands the
other an unforgeable reference to an object, and the other side calls methods on it
across the network. The rule is the one you already trust in local code. If you hold
the reference you can use it, and if you do not, there is nothing to forge. That is
what [OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn), the open object-capability network
protocol, is for, and pancake is a small [Zig](https://ziglang.org/) library that
speaks it.

[Their program hands yours a reference to an object called "greeter." Holding that reference is the whole permission to call it.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-capability.svg'))

Everything else in pancake keeps that promise. The protocol
core is deterministic and synchronous and does no I/O of its own, so the sockets and
the scheduling live in adapters stacked above it. Nothing important hides either.
Where a frame is owned or borrowed, where a copy happens, and which wire format is on
the line all stay visible at the boundary that owns them.

Two peers can reach each other directly, with no server in the middle. The
[iroh transport]($link.page('transports/iroh')) carries a session over encrypted
QUIC and names each peer by a self-authenticating key, so the address you dial *is*
the identity you connected to — one Ed25519 key is at once the network route, the
capability you name, and the key that signs the session. It rides an optional
artifact, so a program that only speaks TCP never pays for it.

You can reach the same library three ways. It is a Zig package you import directly,
it is a plain C ABI you can call from anything, and it is four language bindings that
each feel native in Rust, TypeScript, Python, and Racket.

This site is the front door. The deep documentation lives next to the source, so a
claim about the code travels in the same change as the code itself. Pick a path
below, or wander the modules from the sidebar.
